locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 has "end_continuation" and "young_need"; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 have "end_continuation" and "young_need" and "end_continuation ∧ young_need → O last_while_needed"; they differ when only "the end of conjunction between male and female is not barely procreation but the continuation of the species" and "the young are to be sustained by those that got them till they are able to shift and provide for themselves" are true, which the first allows
locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 with no modality; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 as what ought to be or is permitted: the text does not say which it means
locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the end of conjunction between male and female is not barely procreation but the continuation of the species" and "the young are to be sustained by those that got them till they are able to shift and provide for themselves" are true and "it ought to be that the conjunction betwixt male and female lasts, even after procreation, so long as is necessary to the nourishment and support of the young ones" and "it ought to be that the conjunction betwixt male and female lasts, even after procreation, so long as is necessary to the nourishment and support of the young ones" are false. Is S3, "For the end of conjunction, between male and female, being not barely procreation, but the continuation of the species; thisconjunction betwixt male and female ought to last, even after procreation, so long as is necessary to the nourishment and support of the young ones, who are to be sustained by those that got them, till they are able to shift and provide for themselves.", true then?
locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 have "maker_rule"; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 has "maker_rule" and "creatures_obey"; they differ when only "this rule is one which the infinite wise maker hath set to the works of his hands" is true, which the first allows
locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "this rule is one which the infinite wise maker hath set to the works of his hands" is true and "the inferior creatures steadily obey this rule" is false. Is S4, "This rule, which the infinite wise maker hath set to the works of his hands, we find the inferior creatures steadily obey.", true then?
locke-treatise-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 has "end_continuation ∧ young_need → O last_while_needed" and "grass_short ∧ prey_longer ∧ bird_mates_continue → creatures_obey"; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 has "grass_short ∧ prey_longer ∧ bird_mates_continue → creatures_obey"; they differ when only "the end of conjunction between male and female is not barely procreation but the continuation of the species", "the young are to be sustained by those that got them till they are able to shift and provide for themselves", "the inferior creatures steadily obey this rule", "in viviparous animals which feed on grass, the conjunction lasts no longer than the very act of copulation", "in beasts of prey, the conjunction lasts longer" and "in birds, the cock and hen continue mates till the young are able to use their wing and provide for themselves" are true, which the second allows; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 have "O last_while_needed"; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 has "creatures_obey"; they differ when only "it ought to be that the conjunction betwixt male and female lasts, even after procreation, so long as is necessary to the nourishment and support of the young ones" is true, which the first allows
locke-treatise-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 with no modality; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 as what ought to be or is permitted: the text does not say which it means
locke-treatise-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3 put it at S3, "For the end of conjunction, between male and female, being not barely procreation, but the continuation of the species; thisconjunction betwixt male and female ought to last, even after procreation, so long as is necessary to the nourishment and support of the young ones, who are to be sustained by those that got them, till they are able to shift and provide for themselves."; locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.2 at S4, "This rule, which the infinite wise maker hath set to the works of his hands, we find the inferior creatures steadily obey."
locke-treatise-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-1.logic.r1 and locke-treatise-1.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "For the end of conjunction, between male and female, being not barely procreation, but the continuation of the species; thisconjunction betwixt male and female ought to last, even after procreation, so long as is necessary to the nourishment and support of the young ones, who are to be sustained by those that got them, till they are able to shift and provide for themselves.", rather than S4, "This rule, which the infinite wise maker hath set to the works of his hands, we find the inferior creatures steadily obey."?
